\begin_layout Standard
Let's name by 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{i}$
\end_inset

 the number of unique values produced by 
\begin_inset Formula $2^{i}$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $2^{i}$
\end_inset

produces all values of 
\begin_inset Formula $2^{i-2}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{i}$
\end_inset

 will not include values of 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{i-2}$
\end_inset

 and so on.
 In our example above, 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{10}=12$
\end_inset

 : [1024, 341, 340, 113, 336, 320, 106, 35, 104, 34, 11, 96], 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{8}=6$
\end_inset

 : [256, 85, 84, 80, 26, 24].
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $N_{i}$
\end_inset

 is a determinstic counting function of 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 which grows as 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{i}=\frac{4}{3}N_{i-1}$
\end_inset

(formal demonstration required here).
\end_layout

